{"text": " Your screen time isn't the real problem, your pickups are. And your phone already tells you which app keeps pulling you back. Most people never look. Open screen time on an iPhone or digital wellbeing on Android and find pickups or unlocks. Say it says 90 a day. That's 90 times you didn't decide. If each one costs you two minutes, that's three hours gone in pieces. Now scroll down to first used after pickup. That's the app your thumb opens before you've even thought about it. Say it's Instagram. So what do you do with it? Step one, move that one app off your home screen into a folder on the last page. Not deleted, just three swipes away. Step two, turn off its notifications so it stops calling you. The phone stays, but now there's a pause and the pause is where you get to decide. Check your pickups again in seven days. Here's the reset, find your first app, move it off page one, mute it and check again on day seven. Send this to the friend who checks their phone mid-sentence. That's what Demure is for. You archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. Follow for one fix a day, tonight the coffee that's still in you at bedtime. If you want to try it out, I've put the link in the bio.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.06, "text": " Your screen time isn't the real problem, your pickups are.", "tokens": [50363, 3406, 3159, 640, 2125, 470, 262, 1103, 1917, 11, 534, 46462, 389, 13, 50515], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06710075486636331, "compression_ratio": 1.631578947368421, "no_speech_prob": 0.001814878429286182, "words": [{"word": " Your", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.28, "probability": 0.7655888795852661}, {"word": " screen", "start": 0.28, "end": 0.56, "probability": 0.9342246055603027}, {"word": " time", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.9, "probability": 0.9764124155044556}, {"word": " isn't", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.22, "probability": 0.9887709319591522}, {"word": " the", "st